WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:08.696
We begin by isolating a specific region of
flowing fluid, which we call a control volume.

2
00:00:08.696 --> 00:00:15.107
Imagine drawing a transparent box around a
section of a water pipe.

3
00:00:15.107 --> 00:00:23.642
This boundary is purely imaginary, but it
allows us to track exactly what enters and

4
00:00:23.642 --> 00:00:27.625
leaves without worrying about the fluid
outside.

5
00:00:27.625 --> 00:00:37.681
The control volume concept transforms a
complex, continuous system into a manageable

6
00:00:37.681 --> 00:00:39.357
mathematical framework.

7
00:00:39.357 --> 00:00:47.896
By focusing only on the mass crossing this
imaginary surface, we can apply conservation

8
00:00:47.896 --> 00:00:50.946
laws directly to the flow.

9
00:00:50.946 --> 00:00:59.191
This approach is essential because it
separates the internal dynamics from external

10
00:00:59.191 --> 00:01:04.000
influences, letting us analyze steady
conditions systematically.

11
00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:10.607
We will now examine how mass behaves within
this defined space.

12
00:01:10.754 --> 00:01:19.521
Density measures how much mass occupies a
given volume, acting as the bridge between

13
00:01:19.521 --> 00:01:21.400
mass and space.

14
00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:29.168
In our control volume, density determines how
tightly packed the fluid molecules are.

15
00:01:29.168 --> 00:01:38.382
If we compress the fluid, density increases,
meaning more mass fits into the same volume.

16
00:01:38.382 --> 00:01:44.632
Conversely, heating the fluid expands it,
lowering density.

17
00:01:44.632 --> 00:01:53.329
This property is crucial because it dictates
how the fluid responds to pressure changes and

18
00:01:53.329 --> 00:01:54.489
area variations.

19
00:01:54.489 --> 00:02:02.184
We treat density as a fundamental material
property that remains constant for liquids but

20
00:02:02.184 --> 00:02:04.382
can shift for gases.

21
00:02:04.382 --> 00:02:13.887
Recognizing density allows us to predict how
mass distributes itself within our imaginary

22
00:02:13.887 --> 00:02:16.811
boundary before analyzing motion.

23
00:02:16.970 --> 00:02:24.098
Mass flow rate quantifies how much mass
crosses a plane each second.

24
00:02:24.098 --> 00:02:31.739
Imagine a conveyor belt carrying boxes; the
mass flow rate tells you exactly how many

25
00:02:31.739 --> 00:02:34.795
boxes pass a checkpoint per minute.

26
00:02:34.795 --> 00:02:42.362
Mathematically, we multiply density by the
cross-sectional area and the average velocity

27
00:02:42.362 --> 00:02:44.884
to get this value.

28
00:02:44.884 --> 00:02:54.607
This formula works because density gives mass
per volume, area gives volume per length, and

29
00:02:54.607 --> 00:02:57.848
velocity gives length per time.

30
00:02:57.848 --> 00:03:05.098
When you combine them, volume and length
cancel out, leaving mass per time.

31
00:03:05.098 --> 00:03:12.431
This calculation is foundational because it
converts spatial dimensions into a temporal

32
00:03:12.431 --> 00:03:19.152
rate, allowing us to track material movement
through our control volume.

33
00:03:19.298 --> 00:03:25.748
Continuity enforces mass conservation within
our control volume.

34
00:03:25.748 --> 00:03:33.958
If mass enters at a certain rate, it must exit
at the exact same rate when the flow is

35
00:03:33.958 --> 00:03:34.390
steady.

36
00:03:34.390 --> 00:03:42.926
No mass accumulates inside the box, and no
sources or sinks exist within it.

37
00:03:42.926 --> 00:03:51.860
This principle means the product of density,
area, and velocity remains constant along the

38
00:03:51.860 --> 00:03:52.498
pipe.

39
00:03:52.498 --> 00:04:00.265
If the pipe narrows, the fluid must speed up
to maintain the same mass flow rate.

40
00:04:00.265 --> 00:04:05.962
Conversely, if the pipe widens, the fluid
slows down.

41
00:04:05.962 --> 00:04:13.896
This relationship holds regardless of the
fluid type, provided we account for density

42
00:04:13.896 --> 00:04:16.337
changes in compressible gases.

43
00:04:16.337 --> 00:04:25.984
Continuity is the first law of fluid mechanics
in action, dictating how geometry forces

44
00:04:25.984 --> 00:04:28.051
velocity to adjust.

45
00:04:28.202 --> 00:04:34.794
Pressure in a flowing fluid splits into two
distinct components.

46
00:04:34.794 --> 00:04:43.580
Static pressure measures the fluid's inherent
thermodynamic energy, independent of motion.

47
00:04:43.580 --> 00:04:52.062
Dynamic pressure quantifies the kinetic energy
associated with the fluid's velocity.

48
00:04:52.062 --> 00:05:00.705
When fluid moves faster, dynamic pressure
increases while static pressure typically

49
00:05:00.705 --> 00:05:01.491
decreases.

50
00:05:01.491 --> 00:05:09.294
This trade-off occurs because total energy
remains constant in ideal conditions.

51
00:05:09.294 --> 00:05:17.491
We visualize this by imagining a stationary
observer measuring static pressure with a

52
00:05:17.491 --> 00:05:25.687
wall-mounted gauge, versus a moving observer
experiencing dynamic pressure as a ram effect.

53
00:05:25.687 --> 00:05:33.648
Understanding both components allows us to
predict how forces distribute across pipe

54
00:05:33.648 --> 00:05:38.955
walls and how velocity changes affect pressure
readings.

55
00:05:39.098 --> 00:05:47.228
Bernoulli's equation links static pressure,
dynamic pressure, and total pressure along a

56
00:05:47.228 --> 00:05:47.905
streamline.

57
00:05:47.905 --> 00:05:56.843
It states that the sum of static pressure and
one-half density times velocity squared equals

58
00:05:56.843 --> 00:05:59.226
a constant total pressure.

59
00:05:59.226 --> 00:06:06.980
This relationship emerges from energy
conservation, assuming steady, inviscid,

60
00:06:06.980 --> 00:06:16.458
incompressible flow without heat addition,
shaft work, or significant elevation changes.

61
00:06:16.458 --> 00:06:23.958
These assumptions simplify reality but are
critical for accurate predictions.

62
00:06:23.958 --> 00:06:32.777
If viscosity, compressibility, pumps,
turbines, heat transfer, or unsteady behavior

63
00:06:32.777 --> 00:06:39.833
materially affect the flow, Bernoulli's
simplified form fails.

64
00:06:39.833 --> 00:06:47.369
We must verify these conditions before
applying the equation to real-world systems.

65
00:06:47.522 --> 00:06:56.394
Combining continuity and Bernoulli's equation
reveals how pipe geometry dictates pressure

66
00:06:56.394 --> 00:06:58.007
and velocity.

67
00:06:58.007 --> 00:07:05.257
When a pipe narrows, continuity forces
velocity to increase.

68
00:07:05.257 --> 00:07:14.475
Bernoulli's equation then predicts that this
velocity increase must be offset by a drop in

69
00:07:14.475 --> 00:07:15.704
static pressure.

70
00:07:15.704 --> 00:07:23.561
Conversely, a widening pipe reduces velocity
and raises static pressure.

71
00:07:23.561 --> 00:07:31.797
This inverse relationship between area and
pressure is fundamental to designing ducts,

72
00:07:31.797 --> 00:07:34.543
nozzles, and Venturi meters.

73
00:07:34.543 --> 00:07:41.563
We can visualize this by tracking pressure
gauges along a tapered pipe: readings will

74
00:07:41.563 --> 00:07:47.079
fall in the narrow section and rise in the
wide section.

75
00:07:47.079 --> 00:07:56.186
These relationships allow engineers to predict
flow behavior without complex simulations.

76
00:07:56.330 --> 00:08:02.220
Pitot measurement exploits the
area-velocity-pressure relationship to

77
00:08:02.220 --> 00:08:04.744
determine fluid speed.

78
00:08:04.744 --> 00:08:13.327
A Pitot tube points directly into the flow,
capturing dynamic pressure at its stagnation

79
00:08:13.327 --> 00:08:13.940
point.

80
00:08:13.940 --> 00:08:18.547
A static port measures the ambient static
pressure.

81
00:08:18.547 --> 00:08:26.385
The difference between these two readings
equals dynamic pressure, which we convert to

82
00:08:26.385 --> 00:08:28.797
velocity using Bernoulli's equation.

83
00:08:28.797 --> 00:08:37.530
This technique is widely used in aviation and
HVAC systems to map airflow patterns.

84
00:08:37.530 --> 00:08:47.426
By placing multiple Pitot probes along a duct,
we can reconstruct the velocity profile and

85
00:08:47.426 --> 00:08:49.405
verify theoretical predictions.

86
00:08:49.405 --> 00:08:57.096
The method relies on accurate pressure sensing
and assumes the flow remains undisturbed by

87
00:08:57.096 --> 00:08:58.744
the probe itself.

88
00:08:58.898 --> 00:09:08.147
Real flows rarely meet ideal assumptions,
introducing energy losses that invalidate

89
00:09:08.147 --> 00:09:10.669
simple Bernoulli calculations.

90
00:09:10.669 --> 00:09:19.115
Friction against pipe walls, turbulence, and
sudden expansions dissipate mechanical energy

91
00:09:19.115 --> 00:09:20.651
into heat.

92
00:09:20.651 --> 00:09:28.580
These losses reduce the total pressure
available for flow, meaning static and dynamic

93
00:09:28.580 --> 00:09:32.240
pressure sums will not remain constant.

94
00:09:32.240 --> 00:09:40.238
We must recognize invalid applications when
viscosity, compressibility, pumps, turbines,

95
00:09:40.238 --> 00:09:47.437
heat transfer, or unsteady behavior materially
affect the flow.

96
00:09:47.437 --> 00:09:56.615
In such cases, we switch to the general energy
equation or use empirical loss coefficients.

97
00:09:56.615 --> 00:10:04.548
Identifying these limitations prevents
erroneous predictions and ensures engineering

98
00:10:04.548 --> 00:10:08.955
designs account for real-world inefficiencies.

99
00:10:09.098 --> 00:10:14.315
Let us apply these concepts to a
narrowing-pipe example.

100
00:10:14.315 --> 00:10:20.583
Suppose water flows through a pipe with an
inlet area of 0.

101
00:10:20.583 --> 00:10:26.208
05 square meters at 2 meters per second.

102
00:10:26.208 --> 00:10:29.762
The outlet area shrinks to 0.

103
00:10:29.762 --> 00:10:33.333
025 square meters.

104
00:10:33.333 --> 00:10:41.387
Using continuity, we find the outlet velocity
doubles to 4 meters per second.

105
00:10:41.387 --> 00:10:50.548
Assuming incompressible flow, density remains
1000 kilograms per cubic meter.

106
00:10:50.548 --> 00:10:56.708
Applying Bernoulli's equation, we calculate
the static pressure drop.

107
00:10:56.708 --> 00:11:06.282
The dynamic pressure increases by half density
times the velocity change squared, yielding a

108
00:11:06.282 --> 00:11:08.333
measurable pressure difference.

109
00:11:08.333 --> 00:11:17.358
This step-by-step calculation demonstrates how
area reduction forces velocity increase and

110
00:11:17.358 --> 00:11:23.101
static pressure decrease, completing our
practical analysis.
